ward Tossup and one rates it a Republican flip.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a late break on the cost-of-living and tariff issues could tip this either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Marcy Kaptur (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Marcy Kaptur seeks another term in a northwestern Ohio seat redrawn to lean Republican, facing state legislator Derek Merrin in a rematch. The district's R+5 lean and a competitive national environment make this one of the House's closest races. Expert raters disagree: several call it a Tossup or Lean D, while Inside Elections rates it a Republican flip, and prediction markets give Kaptur roughly a 73% chance.\n\nKaptur holds a decisive financial edge, with $3.5 million on hand to Merrin's $0.5 million, and has run ads emphasizing local projects alongside controversial anti-transgender messaging. Merrin has campaigned with Vivek Ramaswamy on cost-of-living and tariff themes. With no reliable general-election polling, the race rests on fundamentals, pointing to a narrow Democratic advantage with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7474, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7256, "margin": 3.5}]}